---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import SectionHeading from '../components/SectionHeading.astro';
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';
import { SITE } from '../data/site';

const saints = await getCollection('saints');
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About, Methodology & Sources"
  description="How the Communion of Saints Digital Library researches, writes, sources, and credits its content — and the principles that guide it."
  canonicalPath="/about"
  bodyClass="parchment-bg"
>
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-4xl px-4 py-10 sm:px-6">
    <Breadcrumbs crumbs={[{ label: 'Home', href: '/' }, { label: 'About & Sources' }]} />

    <SectionHeading
      label="About this library"
      title="A Sanctuary of Truth and Beauty"
      sub="The Communion of Saints Digital Library exists to make the lives of the Catholic saints known — reverently, accurately, and beautifully."
    />

    <div class="saint-prose mt-10">
      <p>
        The saints are the Church's great gift to the imagination of charity: real men and women
        of every age and every land who lived the gospel with extraordinary fidelity. This
        library gathers their lives — biography, writings, miracles, patronage, symbols, feast
        days, and historical context — into a calm, searchable sanctuary where scholars, students,
        devotees, and the merely curious can dwell.
      </p>

      <h2>Our principles</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Reverence.</strong> We treat the saints as friends of God and models of the Christian life, never as ornaments or kitsch.</li>
        <li><strong>Accuracy.</strong> Dates, feast days, and canonization facts follow the General Roman Calendar and official Church documents.</li>
        <li><strong>Honesty.</strong> Where tradition and history diverge, we say so. Legendary material is labeled as tradition; historical material is cited.</li>
        <li><strong>Breadth.</strong> We include Roman Catholic and Eastern Catholic saints, and note the historic and ecclesial context of each life.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>How we write</h2>
      <p>
        Biographies are <em>original compositions</em>, written in a warm and scholarly voice for a
        general audience. They are researched from public-domain sources — principally the
        <em>Catholic Encyclopedia</em> (1907–1914), older editions of <em>Butler's Lives of the
        Saints</em>, the <em>Acta Sanctorum</em>, the Early Church Fathers collection, and the
        public-domain writings of the saints themselves — and verified against modern official
        sources such as the Vatican website and the USCCB calendar. We never copy modern
        copyrighted pages or books. Direct quotations from the saints and the Scriptures are
        attributed on the page.
      </p>

      <h2>How we source images</h2>
      <p>
        We use only public-domain or openly licensed images from reliable sources — Wikimedia
        Commons, museum open-access collections (such as Google Art Project, the Hermitage, and
        the Met), and approved Catholic media. Every image carries a credit and a source link.
        Where no suitable image exists, a stained-glass style monogram is generated in the
        saint's name and symbol, so the page never appears bare.
      </p>

      <h2>What we do not claim</h2>
      <p>
        We are not an official organ of the Catholic Church. Canonization dates, feast days, and
        devotional titles follow current liturgical norms to the best of our ability and are
        corrected as the Church's calendar develops. This is a labor of love for the communion of
        saints, offered in hope that it may serve the faith of the Church.
      </p>

      <h2>Corrections & contributions</h2>
      <p>
        We welcome corrections and additions. If you find an error of fact, a dated feast day, or
        a missing patron, please write to the editors. Every saint page includes its sources so
        that claims can be checked.
      </p>

      <h2>The library today</h2>
      <p>
        {SITE.name} currently holds <strong>{saints.length} saints</strong>, selected to cover the
        General Roman Calendar, the most-invoked patronages, and the great figures of every
        century — with room to grow, saint by saint, toward a fuller library.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
